{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792921705,"nonce":"QWiHEh8jk9pnqdRCbjFcX/Q3hVM=","title":"First tier-up compiles the whole program synchronously, dominating cold runs","message":"When the first interpreted function crosses interpreter_threshold, TieredBackend::maybe_promote_interpreter_threshold_to_baseline calls compile_all_modules_jit(): on the calling thread, with the program stalled, it runs the deferred O2 pipeline over every module and compiles all of them (Cranelift, or LLVM under --llvm via tier_up_with_llvm), then marks every compiled function Baseline. Per-function promotion through beadie, on its broker thread, exists only for the tiers after that first pass.\n\nCold deltablue on the NUC (i5-1250P), `rayzor run --release`, fresh process, median of 11:\n\n- Cranelift: 161 ms wall. Ready to enter main at 57 ms (frontend 36, MIR optimize 9, backend construction 9). main ran 133 ms, of which 126 ms was the single native pass.\n- --llvm: 368 ms wall, 333 ms of it the single native pass during main.\n\ndeltablue's own work at steady state is about 7 ms, so a cold run is almost entirely that one compile. An Ash comparison on a Mac measured 189 ms (Cranelift) and 405 ms (LLVM) for the same run, against 44-51 ms for Ash plus 130-150 ms for its haxe -hl build.\n\nWhat would remove it: make the first native compile per function and off the calling thread, like the later tiers. The function that turned hot (and, lazily, what it calls) goes to beadie, while the interpreter keeps running and moves to the compiled code through the existing OSR resume variants when it lands. The deferred O2 then runs per compiled function, not over every module.","files":null}]}